{"kind":"propose","text":"Proposal for Hugh's adoption under request 59154b87 (#22093, the remaining oversized-attachment outcome of #137), on current bases at main d70d8fe6. One ratifiable outcome: narrow decision fcb32a61 (#5075). Withdraw its operative sentence, that an attachment may exceed the payload ceiling when the submitter supplies a locator, and keep everything else it adopted: nothing stored implicitly, no implied custody, option 2 of the retired design b72e4767 still rejected, and the better refusal message it required and which never shipped. The delivery under this outcome: (1) keep the kernel's hard 1 MiB genesis ceiling and its enforcement in internal/kernel/kernel.go exactly as it is; (2) replace the bare refusal 'event exceeds genesis ceiling' with a diagnostic that names the ceiling, the measured total, the envelope, payload and attachment split, the largest attachment by name, and the remedy (shrink, split across events, or cite a repository path); (3) add the size preflight the batch path already uses to the three surfaces that today sign and submit before any check, gs state --evidence, MCP state and the resident /v0/act, so they refuse before signing through the already exported kernel.ValidateRequestSize, with retry identity untouched because nothing re-signs; (4) correct the only remaining claim of a degrade path in notes/2026-08-05-gitseq-design.md lines 113 to 115 and the wording at docs/reference/gs/verify.md line 69, and add to docs/reference/limits.md the resident's separate 2 MiB request-body cap with its base64 expansion and the measured attachment distribution below; (5) record this narrowing as the ratifier's abandonment of conversion. Tests and omission controls: refusal-message content at each boundary (envelope only, payload only, one attachment, aggregate); refusal before signing on each of the three surfaces, each with the preflight mutated out to show the test red; unchanged admission below the ceiling. About 150 to 250 lines across internal/kernel, cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp, internal/service and three documents. Why this and not the locator path: the attached assessment walked all 27,746 events of the four current workrooms; 543 carry 1,725 attachments; per-attachment sizes are p50 1,279 bytes, p90 9,096, p99 60,225, max 498,733; the largest event total is 535,397 bytes, 51 percent of the ceiling; no event has ever been refused and no request, document or comment asks for a larger attachment. The two things that would have helped anyone in that record, an actionable message and refusing before signing, need no locator. A locator descriptor would put a signed record naming bytes Gitseq does not hold onto a reading surface (previews and the UI evidence row now exist, so #7447's unreadable-on-arrival premise no longer holds) that has no vocabulary for 'not held', on sha1 workrooms where the digest binds weakly: the implied-custody problem #5075 exists to avoid, moved rather than removed. The counterweight, stated so it is not hidden: the headroom #7447 relied on has largely gone, from nineteen times to two times in five weeks, with growth concentrated in the newest third of the log and the three largest events written on 5 and 6 September 2026; the narrowing is right for the world as measured, not for one at four times this volume, which is why limits.md carries the distribution so the next reader sees the slope, and why the diagnostic must make the next person's decision easy. Kept outside this outcome as explanation only: the locator path in full (costed in the assessment at roughly 400 to 700 lines across about 13 files, with a re-sign and idempotency trap inside the one function that signs everything); raising the genesis ceiling, which needs a new workroom and is excluded by the request; reading attachments back from gs inspect, a separate reading-surface outcome; and any wider treatment of the resident cap beyond the one limits.md line. Basis note: #5075 is live and twice ratified effective by Hugh but stale because the design it answered, b72e4767, is retired; that staleness propagates to #22093 and my promise, which is why this goes through ordinary proposal and adoption rather than the authority-bearing chain. Adoption of this proposal is the authorization for the delivery; I will not change source before it is ratified. If Hugh prefers the locator path, decline this and I will file that as its own one-outcome proposal from the same assessment.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59154b8746afa9148f9c48026b269b6fdaabc8b1, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c2c186516fa6f2df92a9b2d23f605c5bf744c388, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fcb32a61ec6415eda5d909b4db7e215a07c1dc8a stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015bb3acec403ed0d43bcd2a3c38bd188f7740ef, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02bae1e811fde13104ef614631ac608dca7a7295 and 171 more","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}